specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:7:
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:8:Every tool registered in `src/orchestration/tools/registry.ts` declares a JSON Schema `inputSchema` that is forwarded to the configured AI provider, and `ToolRegistry.execute` validates the model-supplied `input` against that schema before calling the tool's executor. When validation fails, `execute` returns a rejection `ToolResponse` describing what was wrong rather than throwing, so the orchestrator feeds the failure back to the model as a `tool_result` within the existing tool-round budget.
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:9:
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:10:## Validation at execute time
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:11:
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:12:- A missing required property is rejected before the executor runs, and the returned result identifies that property as required ([validated by: rejects input missing a required property and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L20)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:13:- When `input` omits a property listed in `required`, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the missing property as required and never invokes the executor ([validated by: rejects input missing a required property with a ToolResponse and never calls the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L16)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:14:- When `input` supplies a property whose value does not match its declared JSON Schema type, `ToolRegistry.execute` returns a `ToolResponse` naming the failing field path and expected type without invoking the executor, and neither `result` nor the emitted log record contains the offending property value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property with its field path and expected type without exposing its value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L33); [validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property naming the field and expected type without leaking the offending value to the result or logs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L46)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:15:- A nested wrong-typed property is reported with its complete field path ([validated by: reports a nested invalid property path](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L48)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:16:- A root property with the wrong type is reported by its field name and expected type ([validated by: names a root property when its value has the wrong type](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.test.ts#L59)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:17:- When `input` satisfies `required`, declared property types and `enum`, `ToolRegistry.execute` invokes the executor once and passes the input object through unchanged — including undeclared extra properties — without injecting `additionalProperties: false`, coercing types or applying defaults.
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:18:- When `ToolRegistry.execute` is called with a tool name that has not been registered, it throws `Unknown tool: <name>` because an unregistered tool is a programming or configuration error rather than a recoverable input mistake.
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:19:
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:20:## Schema compilation and caching
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:21:
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:22:- A static `ToolDefinition` has its `inputSchema` compiled once at `register` time, so a schema that is not valid JSON Schema throws synchronously from `register` with an error message naming the offending tool ([validated by: throws at register time naming the tool when a static inputSchema cannot be compiled](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L94)).
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:23:- A static tool's `inputSchema` is compiled at `register` time and reused on every `execute` call without re-reading or re-compiling `definition.inputSchema`.
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:24:- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` resolves its definition on each `execute` call and validates against the `inputSchema` returned for that call, caching compiled validators by schema object reference (`WeakMap`) so distinct returned schemas each enforce their own rules.
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:25:
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:26:## Orchestrator tool-loop recovery
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:27:
specs/hal-engine-tool-input-validation/spec.md:28:- Because a schema rejection returns a `ToolResponse` instead of throwing, `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` sends the rejection `result` back to the provider as a `tool_result` message, allowing the model to issue a corrected tool call on the next round and complete the turn within `maxToolRounds` ([validated by: returns a schema rejection as a tool_result in the orchestrator loop so the model can correct the call on the next round](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L112)).
